---
import Post from "../../layouts/Post.astro";
import { SITE, DEFAULT_OG, iso, POSTS, authorNode, publisherNode, websiteNode, topicsForTags } from "../../lib/blog.mjs";

const slug = "freelancer-vs-agency-web-development";
const idx = POSTS.findIndex((p) => p.slug === slug);
const post = POSTS[idx];

const canonical = `${SITE}/blog/${slug}/`;
const ogImage = post.coverImage ? `${SITE}${post.coverImage}` : DEFAULT_OG;
const primaryTag = post.tags[0] || "Notes";
const readMins = post.readMins;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Should I hire a freelancer or an agency?",
    a: "Depends on the size of the job and your tolerance for single-person risk. A senior independent is usually faster and cheaper because there's no account layer between you and the person building \u2014 you talk to whoever writes the code. An agency is worth its overhead when the project needs several specialists at once, or when your organisation requires the structure.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is hiring a freelancer riskier than an agency?",
    a: "There's real key-person risk, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest. You reduce it by insisting on things that make you portable: code in a repository you own, hosting in your name, documentation good enough for someone else to continue. Do that and the worst case is inconvenient rather than catastrophic \u2014 and it's worth noting agencies fold too.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does an agency's higher price actually buy?",
    a: "Sometimes genuine specialisation \u2014 a researcher, a designer, a developer and a strategist who are each properly good at their part. Sometimes it buys account management, sales commission and office rent. Both are priced the same way from outside, so the question worth asking is which specific people will touch your project.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I vet an independent developer?",
    a: "Visit their live work on your own phone. Ask what they'd change about a project they've shipped \u2014 anyone honest has an answer. Ask what they'd refuse to build. Then start with something small if you can; how someone handles a two-week job tells you almost everything about how they'd handle a three-month one.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if the freelancer disappears mid-project?",
    a: "It's the fair worry. Mitigate it structurally rather than by trusting harder: pay in milestones tied to delivered work, keep the repository in your own account from day one, and hold your own domain and hosting. Then a disappearance costs you time and a handover rather than the whole project.",
  },
];

const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      "@id": `${canonical}#post`,
      headline: post.title,
      description: post.description,
      datePublished: iso(post.pubDate),
      dateModified: iso(post.updatedDate || post.pubDate),
      author: authorNode(SITE),
      publisher: publisherNode(SITE),
      isPartOf: websiteNode(SITE),
      mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": canonical },
      image: ogImage,
      articleSection: primaryTag,
      keywords: post.tags.join(", "),
      // Resolves the free-text tags above to public concept identifiers, so a
      // parser knows "Astro" means the framework and "GEO" isn't geography.
      about: topicsForTags(post.tags),
      wordCount: post.wordCount,
      timeRequired: `PT${readMins}M`,
      speakable: {
        "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
        cssSelector: [".post-title", ".post-lead"],
      },
      inLanguage: "en",
      url: canonical,
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Blog", item: `${SITE}/blog/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: post.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
    {
      // FAQ rich results were retired by Google in May 2026, so this earns no
      // SERP feature. It stays because answer engines still parse it and every
      // question below is rendered visibly on the page.
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ "@type": "Question", name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a } })),
    },
  ],
});
---

<Post post={post} jsonld={jsonld} faqs={faqs}>
<p>Here’s a story I tell on sales calls, because it decides most of them.</p>
<p>A school needed a website and an admissions portal. The agencies they approached quoted six months. I shipped both — public site plus an admin panel handling admissions for classes I–XII — in <a href="/projects/modernkbs/">seven days</a>.</p>
<p>That’s not because agencies are bad. It’s because you were quoted for an <em>organization</em>, and sometimes what your project needs is a <em>person</em>. Knowing which is the entire decision.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-an-agency-invoice-actually-pays-for">What an agency invoice actually pays for</h2>
<p>When an agency quotes five times a freelancer’s price, the difference isn’t greed. You’re buying:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Coordination.</strong> A project manager, a strategist, a designer, developers, QA — and the meetings that keep them aligned.</li>
<li><strong>Redundancy.</strong> If someone quits mid-project, the agency absorbs it. A freelancer who disappears is your problem.</li>
<li><strong>Breadth.</strong> Brand strategy, a photo shoot, a video, paid media, a 60-page site — one contract, many disciplines.</li>
<li><strong>Accountability theater.</strong> Contracts, SLAs, status decks. Sometimes your procurement team genuinely needs these.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a large rebrand with many workstreams, that overhead is the product. Buy it.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="what-youre-not-told">What you’re not told</h2>
<p>The uncomfortable part: at many agencies, the senior people close the deal and juniors do the work. Your project passes through five pairs of hands, and every handoff loses information — the design intent, the copy nuance, the performance budget. Six months isn’t build time; it’s coordination time.</p>
<p>With a senior independent, the person you talk to is the person who designs, codes and ships. Nothing is lost in translation because there is no translation. That’s how seven days happens — it’s not heroics, it’s the absence of handoffs.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="when-each-one-actually-wins">When each one actually wins</h2>
<p><strong>Choose an agency when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The scope spans disciplines a single person can’t credibly cover — brand strategy, film, media buying, a massive site.</li>
<li>Your organization needs vendor redundancy and formal process to sign off.</li>
<li>Budget is genuinely not the constraint, and internal risk-cover matters more than speed.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choose a senior freelancer when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The deliverable is a website, store, landing page or web app — one discipline, done deeply.</li>
<li>Speed matters. Weeks-to-live changes your quarter.</li>
<li>You want direct access to the maker — decisions in one conversation, not routed through an account manager.</li>
<li>You want senior work at a price that isn’t underwriting an office.</li>
</ul>
<p>There is a third arrangement worth knowing about: agencies that keep the client relationship and bring in an independent to build. If you run an agency and that is the shape you need, I do that as a <a href="/agencies/">white-label build partner</a> under your brand.</p>
<p><strong>Avoid both</strong> — the cheap end: marketplaces racing to the bottom, and “agencies” that are one reseller subcontracting to the lowest bidder. The symptom is identical in both cases: nobody senior ever touches your project.</p>
<hr>
<h2 id="how-to-vet-a-freelancer-like-a-pro">How to vet a freelancer like a pro</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Live URLs, not dribbble shots.</strong> Ask for sites in production and click them on your phone. Speed and polish are unfakeable there.</li>
<li><strong>Named clients with outcomes.</strong> “Bookings went up” with a client who’ll confirm it beats any portfolio aesthetic.</li>
<li><strong>A written scope within 48 hours.</strong> Seniors tell you what ships, what it costs and what they’d cut. Vagueness now is vagueness forever.</li>
<li><strong>Who owns the code?</strong> The right answer is you, in writing, with the final invoice.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<p><em>I’m Divyansh Sood — an independent developer shipping agency-scope builds solo: stores, web apps, landing pages and SEO/GEO, <a href="/services/">four services, one person</a>, live in weeks. Agencies also hire me quietly as their build partner, so I’ve seen both sides of this invoice. If you’re deciding between the two for a real project, <a href="mailto:hello@divyanshsood.com">email me the brief</a> — if it genuinely needs an agency, I’ll say so.</em></p>
</Post>
